  "content": {
    "text": "U.S.-Iran Tensions, Inflation Data and Oracle Earnings Dominate Market Focus: Dow Jones, S&P, Nasdaq, Wall Street Futures\nU.S. equity futures traded lower on Wednesday as investors assessed renewed military action between the United States and Iran while awaiting a closely watched inflation report and quarterly results from software giant Oracle (NYSE:ORCL).\nMarkets were also digesting the launch of Anthropic's latest artificial intelligence model, a revised version of its previously restricted \"Mythos-class\" technology that now includes additional safety controls for broader public use.\nFutures Slip Ahead of Key Economic Data\nBy early morning trading, futures linked to the major U.S. indices were pointing lower. Dow futures declined 81 points, or 0.2%, while S&P 500 futures lost 0.3% and Nasdaq 100 futures fell 0.5%.\nWall Street finished mixed in the previous session, with technology stocks once again coming under pressure. Major semiconductor companies including Nvidia, Micron, Intel and Qualcomm all ended the day lower, reflecting ongoing investor concerns about the sustainability of the artificial intelligence-driven rally.\nRenewed Military Action Raises Geopolitical Concerns\nInvestor sentiment continued to be influenced by developments in the Middle East. The United States launched new missile strikes against Iranian targets on Tuesday following an attack on an American helicopter near the Strait of Hormuz.\nPresident Donald Trump blamed Iran for the incident and stated that the U.S. \"must, of necessity, respond.\"\nIran has denied responsibility for the attack and warned that no military action against it would go unanswered.\nAccording to U.S. Central Command, the strikes targeted Iranian air defence systems and satellite radar facilities, although the full extent of the damage remains unclear. Meanwhile, Israel continued military operations in southern Lebanon against Hezbollah positions backed by Iran.\nInflation Report Could Shape Federal Reserve Expectations\nThe latest military developments come despite ongoing efforts to preserve a fragile ceasefire and revive negotiations aimed at reopening the Strait of Hormuz.\nSome market strategists believe the recent escalation may still fit within a broader pattern of limited confrontation followed by diplomatic engagement, reducing the likelihood of a wider regional conflict.\nBrent crude prices remained relatively stable but continue to trade well above levels seen before the conflict began. The Strait of Hormuz remains a key concern for energy markets, given that roughly one-fifth of global oil supplies normally pass through the waterway.\nAgainst this backdrop, investors are closely watching the release of U.S. consumer price index data. Concerns persist that higher energy costs could reignite inflationary pressures and potentially influence monetary policy decisions by major central banks.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "U.S.-Iran Tensions, Inflation Data and Oracle Earnings Dominate Market Focus: Dow Jones, S&P, Nasdaq, Wall Street Futures",
      "U.S. equity futures traded lower on Wednesday as investors assessed renewed military action between the United States and Iran while awaiting a closely watched inflation report and quarterly results from software giant Oracle (NYSE:ORCL).",
      "Markets were also digesting the launch of Anthropic's latest artificial intelligence model, a revised version of its previously restricted \"Mythos-class\" technology that now includes additional safety controls for broader public use.",
      "Futures Slip Ahead of Key Economic Data",
      "By early morning trading, futures linked to the major U.S. indices were pointing lower. Dow futures declined 81 points, or 0.2%, while S&P 500 futures lost 0.3% and Nasdaq 100 futures fell 0.5%.",
      "Wall Street finished mixed in the previous session, with technology stocks once again coming under pressure. Major semiconductor companies including Nvidia, Micron, Intel and Qualcomm all ended the day lower, reflecting ongoing investor concerns about the sustainability of the artificial intelligence-driven rally.",
      "Renewed Military Action Raises Geopolitical Concerns",
      "Investor sentiment continued to be influenced by developments in the Middle East. The United States launched new missile strikes against Iranian targets on Tuesday following an attack on an American helicopter near the Strait of Hormuz.",
      "President Donald Trump blamed Iran for the incident and stated that the U.S. \"must, of necessity, respond.\"",
      "Iran has denied responsibility for the attack and warned that no military action against it would go unanswered.",
      "According to U.S. Central Command, the strikes targeted Iranian air defence systems and satellite radar facilities, although the full extent of the damage remains unclear. Meanwhile, Israel continued military operations in southern Lebanon against Hezbollah positions backed by Iran.",
      "Inflation Report Could Shape Federal Reserve Expectations",
      "The latest military developments come despite ongoing efforts to preserve a fragile ceasefire and revive negotiations aimed at reopening the Strait of Hormuz.",
      "Some market strategists believe the recent escalation may still fit within a broader pattern of limited confrontation followed by diplomatic engagement, reducing the likelihood of a wider regional conflict.",
      "Brent crude prices remained relatively stable but continue to trade well above levels seen before the conflict began. The Strait of Hormuz remains a key concern for energy markets, given that roughly one-fifth of global oil supplies normally pass through the waterway.",
      "Against this backdrop, investors are closely watching the release of U.S. consumer price index data. Concerns persist that higher energy costs could reignite inflationary pressures and potentially influence monetary policy decisions by major central banks."
